import{_ as o,o as t,c as r,a2 as a}from"./chunks/framework.hw3Av5Vn.js";const s="/llamatron/assets/chords_generator.DdC-MO45.svg",n="/llamatron/assets/chords_editor.CLLeSXSe.svg",u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Chords","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"generators/CHORDS.md","filePath":"generators/CHORDS.md"}'),h={name:"generators/CHORDS.md"};function d(i,e,c,l,p,g){return t(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="chords" tabindex="-1">Chords <a class="header-anchor" href="#chords" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chords&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A Chords generator moves a part&#39;s notes to chords and voices them. Write a short figure over chord I, a riff, an arpeggio, a bass line or a chord, and its Mode decides where the chords come from:</p><ul><li><strong>Voicing</strong>, the default, leaves the notes where they are and only voices the chords, as Voice leading below describes.</li><li><strong>Progression</strong> moves the clip&#39;s notes to each chord of a progression in turn, so that one bar becomes a whole section, and changing the progression rewrites the section without touching the pattern.</li><li><strong>Follow Progression</strong> moves the clip&#39;s notes to the chords another track plays, so that a song&#39;s chords are written once, in one place, and everything else follows them.</li><li><strong>AutoChord</strong> turns each single note into a chord, so that any instrument, a built-in synth too, plays chords from a line of single notes. From scale, the default, builds the triad the clip&#39;s scale builds on the note, major, minor, diminished or augmented as the scale says, and leaves a note outside the scale a single note; Major, Minor, Augmented, Diminished and Power build that chord on the note whatever the scale. A chord already written is left as it is. As the Chords tab of every track and clip, it runs before every other generator.</li></ul><p>Every mode then voices the chords if Voice leading is on: on by default in Voicing mode, off in the others until switched on. A drum pattern is left alone.</p><p><img src="'+s+'" alt="A Chords generator&#39;s settings"></p><h2 id="settings" tabindex="-1">Settings <a class="header-anchor" href="#settings" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Settings&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Name</td><td><code>ENTER</code> renames it, and every chain using it follows.</td></tr><tr><td>Mode</td><td>Voicing, Progression, Follow Progression or AutoChord. The columns below it change with the mode.</td></tr><tr><td>Every</td><td>Progression mode: how long each chord lasts, <code>1 bar</code> by default.</td></tr><tr><td>Chords</td><td>Progression mode: the chords as Roman numerals in the song&#39;s scale. <code>ENTER</code> opens the chord editor.</td></tr><tr><td>Chords from</td><td>Follow Progression mode: the track whose chords are followed, a track of the current song. Renaming the track renames this too, and a track the song doesn&#39;t have moves nothing.</td></tr><tr><td>Chord</td><td>AutoChord mode: From scale, the default, Major, Minor, Augmented, Diminished or Power.</td></tr><tr><td>Note on top</td><td>AutoChord mode: off by default. On, the chord ends on the note, its other notes an octave below, so that a melody keeps its line.</td></tr><tr><td>Voice leading</td><td>Every mode: on, each chord after the first takes the inversion and octave whose notes move least from the chord before. On by default in Voicing mode alone.</td></tr><tr><td>Lowest octave, Highest octave</td><td>The chords stay within these octaves, shown in the set&#39;s octave labels, so that they don&#39;t drift up or down over a long piece. A chord no voicing fits plays as written.</td></tr><tr><td>Keep the root in the bass</td><td>Off by default. On, each chord&#39;s lowest written note stays where it is and only the notes above it are voiced, always above it, as a keyboard player plays beside a bass player. <code>Space</code> or <code>ENTER</code> toggles it.</td></tr><tr><td>Restart</td><td>When it starts over, as <a href="./../GENERATORS#restart">Generators</a> describes; with &quot;whole song&quot; a progression runs on across scenes, and with &quot;on new scene&quot; or &quot;whole song&quot; a scene&#39;s first chord is voiced near the last chord before.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="progression" tabindex="-1">Progression <a class="header-anchor" href="#progression" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Progression&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="chord-movement" tabindex="-1">Chord Movement <a class="header-anchor" href="#chord-movement" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chord Movement&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The chords are degrees of the scale. A plain degree moves every note that many steps up the scale, less one, so the scale decides whether the chord is major or minor: in C major, a C major chord moved to 6 plays A minor, and in C minor the same progression is minor. A degree with a sharp or a flat moves the notes by exact half steps instead, keeping the figure&#39;s own sound, which is how borrowed chords such as the rock ♭VII (B♭ major in C major) come out right. A note the pattern writes outside the scale keeps its distance from the scale note below it, so a figure written as a <code>dom7</code> chord stays a dominant seventh on every chord. The rhythm, velocity and everything else stay as written, and a drum pattern is left alone. Each chord lasts Every, a bar by default, counted from the clip&#39;s start, the progression looping for as long as the clip plays.</p><h3 id="chord-editor" tabindex="-1">Chord Editor <a class="header-anchor" href="#chord-editor" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chord Editor&quot;">​</a></h3><p><img src="'+n+`" alt="The chords of a progression, as numerals and names in the song&#39;s
scale"></p><p>The chord editor draws each chord as a column: a bar rising to its degree, then its Roman numeral (upper case for major, lower case for minor, ° for diminished) and its name in the song&#39;s scale, such as <code>Am</code>, then a <code>#/b</code> row, the Quality, Tens. and Scale rows, and an <code>Inv.</code> row. The status line names the chord under the cursor and its notes. A new progression has one chord, I, which changes nothing until edited.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Arrows</td><td>Left and Right move along the chords, Up and Down between the rows, stopping at the edges.</td></tr><tr><td><code>[</code>, <code>]</code></td><td>On the Chord row, step the degree from 1 to 7; on the <code>#/b</code> and <code>Inv.</code> rows, step through the choices, as <code>Space</code> and <code>ENTER</code> do; on the Quality, Tens. and Scale rows, step through the choices and stop at the first and the last.</td></tr><tr><td><code>ENTER</code></td><td>On the Quality, Tens. and Scale rows, opens the <a href="./../PATTERNS#chord-selector">Chord Selector</a>, the one the pattern editor opens, on that row&#39;s list; <code>Space</code> steps to the next choice and <code>Bksp</code> clears.</td></tr><tr><td>Digits</td><td>Type a degree, or an inversion.</td></tr><tr><td><code>I</code>, <code>D</code></td><td>Insert a chord I before the cursor&#39;s, or remove the cursor&#39;s, keeping 1 to 32 chords.</td></tr><tr><td><code>H</code></td><td>Opens a picker of 24 named progressions, from Pop to Andalusian, and fills the chords with the one chosen, matched to the song&#39;s scale.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>An inversion of 1 moves each chord&#39;s lowest note up an octave, and 2 its lowest two.</p><h3 id="chords-outside-the-scale" tabindex="-1">Chords Outside the Scale <a class="header-anchor" href="#chords-outside-the-scale" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chords Outside the Scale&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Three rows let a chord leave the song&#39;s scale. Each chord is worked out in one order: the Scale row picks the scale, the Chord and <code>#/b</code> rows pick the root in that scale, the Quality row names the chord if set, and the Tens. row adds notes on top.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Row</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Scale</td><td>A scale type on the song&#39;s root that this chord borrows, blank for the song&#39;s own. In C major, chord 6 with the Scale &quot;Minor&quot; is A flat major, and the notes of the pattern between the chord&#39;s notes come from C minor too while the chord plays. Because the root is always the song&#39;s, a change of root carries every borrowed chord with it.</td></tr><tr><td>Quality</td><td>A named chord in place of the one the scale builds, blank for the scale&#39;s own. In C major, chord 3 is E minor, and with the Quality <code>M</code> it is E major: the pattern&#39;s notes that are the chord&#39;s third, fifth and seventh are moved to match.</td></tr><tr><td>Tens.</td><td>Tensions: <code>b9</code>, <code>9</code>, <code>#9</code>, <code>11</code>, <code>#11</code>, <code>b13</code> and <code>13</code>. Each sets the pattern&#39;s note that lies between the chord&#39;s notes, and is added on top of a chord the pattern plays.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="follow-progression" tabindex="-1">Follow Progression <a class="header-anchor" href="#follow-progression" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Follow Progression&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="chords-track" tabindex="-1">Chords Track <a class="header-anchor" href="#chords-track" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chords Track&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Put the song&#39;s chords on a track of their own, called Chords here, and turn that track&#39;s Mute on in the track editor so that the chords are a guide and make no sound. In each scene, the Chords track&#39;s clip says what the chords are, in either of two ways. The chords can be written by hand, a step a chord, with the step&#39;s Degree as the chord&#39;s root and its Chord row as the chord. Or the pattern can hold one chord and a Chords generator in Progression mode, on that clip&#39;s Chords tab or in its chain, can make the progression from it. FollowChords reads the chords as they come out of the Chords track, after that track&#39;s own generators, so the two ways are followed alike.</p><h3 id="following" tabindex="-1">Following <a class="header-anchor" href="#following" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Following&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Write a figure over chord I, a bar of bass, an arpeggio or a held chord, and switch on its clip&#39;s Chords tab in Follow Progression mode, or add a Chords generator in that mode to a chain on the clip. As each note starts, it finds the chord most lately started on the track followed, in the same scene, and moves the note up the clip&#39;s scale by as many notes as that chord&#39;s root is above the scale&#39;s root. The scale decides whether a chord is major or minor, as in Progression mode: a figure written on A in A minor plays F, A and C over a chord on F. Where the track followed plays a chord from outside the scale, such as E major in A minor, a note of the figure that is a third, a fifth or a seventh above the figure&#39;s root takes the chord&#39;s own note, so the figure plays G sharp. Rests, rhythm, velocity and gates stay as written.</p><p>A chord goes on through the rests after it, until the next chord starts. Before the first chord of a scene, with no track chosen, or in a scene where the track followed has no clip, nothing moves. A drum pattern is left alone. Tracks cannot follow each other in a circle.</p><p>Change a chord on the Chords track and every clip that follows it changes with it.</p><h2 id="voice-leading" tabindex="-1">Voice Leading <a class="header-anchor" href="#voice-leading" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Voice Leading&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Chords played in root position jump around: C, then G, then Am, each built up from its root, moves every note a long way. A pianist plays G with its B at the bottom and F with its C at the bottom, so that the notes barely move. Voice leading does that for you: the first chord of a clip plays as written, and each chord after takes the inversion and the octave whose notes move least from the chord before. Single notes, such as a bass line&#39;s, are left alone.</p><h2 id="applying-the-generator" tabindex="-1">Applying the Generator <a class="header-anchor" href="#applying-the-generator" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Applying the Generator&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Switch on the Chords tab of a clip or a track, or add the Chords generator to a chain, as <code>chords: name</code>, and give clips that chain; <a href="./../CONCEPTS#chord-progressions">Concepts</a> walks through it.</p><ul><li><code>examples/learning/06_chord_progression_generator.yml</code> plays three one-bar patterns through five progressions, and <code>examples/learning/07_blues_chords.yml</code> is a twelve-bar blues.</li><li><code>examples/learning/05_voice_leading.yml</code> plays three progressions in root position, inverted by hand with the chord editor&#39;s Inv. row, and voice-led; the last scene of <code>examples/learning/06_chord_progression_generator.yml</code> voice-leads its pad.</li><li><code>examples/learning/22_follow_chords.yml</code> plays a band stuck on one chord and then following one muted track, SILENT Chords, one player at a time, with the chords written by hand and then made by a progression.</li></ul>`,30)])])}const f=o(h,[["render",d]]);export{u as __pageData,f as default};
